  "title": "Multiplicatively Weighted Centers of Median Graphs in Quasilinear Time",
  "description": "<p>A short capped integral-distance threshold transformation converts multiplicatively weighted vertex-center feasibility to additively weighted eccentricities. Combining it with the credited Berge-Ducoffe-Habib additive algorithm and Ducoffe's known implicit optimization mechanism gives a deterministic O(n log^5(2n)) exact-operation algorithm for a center, optimum radius and all centers on every finite connected simple unit-edge median graph supplied by adjacency lists and promised median. This resolves the finite algorithmic form of Open Problem 1 in Ducoffe's Oberwolfach Report 8/2026, without a cube-dimension bound. Nonnegative finite real profiles are allowed in an exact comparison model; no encoding-independent bit bound, graph recognition, arbitrary edge-length, infinite-encoding or local-unimodality result is claimed.</p><p>The note credits the earlier cube-free O(n log^2 n) result and makes no novelty claim for the additive algorithm or weighted-median search. Its appendix and verification package give explicit consistent balance-parameter and depth-estimate corrections for two proof issues in arXiv:2410.10235v1, preserving the imported time bound; no claim is made about uninspected final SODA typesetting. Two independent primary-source priority audits found no earlier explicit same-scope resolution, but historical firstness and continued open status are not certified. Inaccessible older tree-center originals, uninspected final SODA typesetting and incomplete indexing bound the audit. The authored-only package preserves original inputs, independent derivations, exact rational finite controls, ordinary nested checksum manifests and an offline replay driver. Brute reference routines are not a fast implementation or an experimental proof of the asymptotic theorem.</p><p>AI tools were used extensively in solving, development, writing, reproduction and adversarial verification. This is an unrefereed preprint with internal AI adversarial reviews; it has not received human peer review or proof-assistant certification.</p>",
